><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>The ability to refine postexercise recovery interventions to optimize&#xD;short-term performance, hormone, and perceptual responses&#xD;after resistance exercise has ramifications for training&#xD;and competition preparation in athletes. The resistance exercise&#xD;session induced significant temporal changes in measures of&#xD;neuromuscular performance, hormone concentration, and&#xD;perceived recovery status. When practitioners seek to optimize&#xD;muscular adaptation (e.g., during a preparatory phase), or recovery&#xD;and performance (e.g., during an in-season competition&#xD;phase), postexercise HWI may assist with providing improvements&#xD;in perceived recovery status (i.e., sleep quality and nextday&#xD;fatigue) and increases in circulating testosterone concentration&#xD;for up to 38 hours after the completion of resistance&#xD;exercise. The aforementioned findings, combinedwith trivial to&#xD;small improvements in neuromuscular performance, may indicate&#xD;a potential adaptive response after resistance exercise&#xD;that may be accrued after repeated HWI exposures over prolonged&#xD;periods. Otherwise, when practitioners seek to maximize&#xD;neuromuscular performance, e.g., during an in-season&#xD;phase before competition, any postexercise water immersion&#xD;strategy (i.e., CWI, CWT, or HWI) may be prescribed to provide&#xD;improvements in acute neuromuscular performance.&#xD;Practitioners working with elite athletes should consider individual&#xD;physiological responses and “preferences” to different&#xD;postexercise water immersion strategies. Practitioners should&#xD;tailor specific strategies on a case-by-case basis to optimize&#xD;outcomes in perceived well-being and neuromuscular performance&#xD;after resistance exercise. Future research should investigate&#xD;the effects of repeated postexercisewater immersion in&#xD;elite and subelite athletes to understand the impact these acute&#xD;findings will have on medium to long-term neuromuscular&#xD;performance, hormone, and perceived well-being responses&#xD;after resistance exercise.</span
></p
></body
